{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788285387187,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9adf9372db07f076","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git merge-base --is-ancestor d9896ec origin/main && echo \"d9896ec IS ancestor of origin/main\" || echo \"d9896ec NOT ancestor\"; git log --oneline origin/main -3"},"output":"d9896ec NOT ancestor\na678620 Land issue-166: per-file definition origin + visibility, module-routing spike\nd676e5f Board: add http-query-sugar-design decide row from composed maintainer note\n08ac4fc Land to-merge-1788204752: promote 1 commits (2 changed lines)\n","metadata":{"output":"d9896ec NOT ancestor\na678620 Land issue-166: per-file definition origin + visibility, module-routing spike\nd676e5f Board: add http-query-sugar-design decide row from composed maintainer note\n08ac4fc Land to-merge-1788204752: promote 1 commits (2 changed lines)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git merge-base --is-ancestor d9896ec origin/main && echo \"d9896ec IS ancestor of origin/main\" || echo \"d9896ec NOT ancestor\"; git log --oneline origin/main -3","time":{"start":1788285387184,"end":1788285387186}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So ESCALATION.md is committed in d9896ec. Resetting to origin/main will drop d9896ec, which removes ESCALATION.md automatically. But wait, is ESCALATION.md tracked at origin/main? Let me check. Actually, I need to reset to origin/main. But git reset --hard requires the file to be committed (it is). So `git reset --hard origin/main` will drop d9896ec and thus ESCALATION.md. But `rm` is denied - but reset --hard handles it.\n\nWait, but the brief says \"delete the stale ESCALATION.md\" and mentions that rm is a known denial. So I should use git reset to handle it since ESCALATION.md is committed in d9896ec.\n\nBut wait - the uncommitted work: src/offload.rs, scratch_off.toy, modified src/lib.rs and src/main.rs. If I do `git reset --hard origin/main`, it will discard the uncommitted changes too! I need to keep the uncommitted work.\n\nHmm, this is a problem. The brief says \"There is uncommitted work already sitting in the worktree (src/offload.rs, scratch_off.toy, modified src/lib.rs and src/main.rs) that looks like real progress on THIS lane's actual task -- inspect it before discarding anything, keep what applies after the reset, drop what doesn't.\"\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Reset branch to origin/main (dropping d9896ec) - this also removes ESCALATION.md\n2. Keep the uncommitted work (src/offload.rs, modifications to lib.rs and main.rs)\n3. scratch_off.toy - decide whether to keep (probably a scratch file, drop it)\n\nSo the approach: stash the uncommitted changes, reset to origin/main, then pop the stash. Or, since the reset is --hard, I should stash first. Let me stash including untracked.\n\nActually wait - but there's a subtlety. The worktree was created from d9896ec presumably. origin/main may have diverged. Let me check the relationship between d9896ec and origin/main. The brief says \"gh:162's module-routing research already landed on main via issue-166 (a678620)\". So origin/main already contains that research, so d9896ec should be... let me check. Is d9896ec an ancestor of origin/main?\n\nLet me check git log of origin/main.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e1d79f20015Wd6uFpcMJVtrM","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","messageID":"msg_05e1d58b4001gRiroWHTT9no1m"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788285387571,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a0b441c21099b79e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src/offload.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src/offload.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The `--explain-offload` diagnostic: which sub-expressions are offloadable and which\n2: //! fall back to scalar evaluation, and why.\n3: //!\n4: //! Vectorizability is silent and cardinality-derived (plans/questions.md Q8): a\n5: //! sub-expression is admissible for offload exactly when its cardinality is known --\n6: //! `One`, `Opt`, or a `fold` over a collection -- and not admissible when it is a\n7: //! `Stream`, whose extent is unbounded. The design's offload boundary and its layer\n8: //! boundary are the same boundary (draft.md, \"Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility\n9: //! predicate\"): everything in the value layer is a kernel candidate, and the effect\n10: //! layer is exactly what cannot be one.\n11: //!\n12: //! This is diagnostic-only. It computes nothing the backends do not already decide from\n13: //! the same types, and it changes neither the type system nor any signature. The flag\n14: //! just makes the otherwise-silent decision visible.\n15: \n16: use crate::tir::{Kind, Program, Tir};\n17: use crate::ty::Type;\n18: \n19: /// The cardinality of an expression, read off its type. Admissibility is a property of\n20: /// the type, not of the operation (draft.md, \"The admissible input set\").\n21: #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n22: enum Cardinality {\n23:     /// Exactly one value per input: a scalar.\n24:     One,\n25:     /// Zero or one: an Opt, admissible as stream compaction.\n26:     Opt,\n27:     /// A collection of known extent: a fold over a Vec.\n28:     Fold,\n29:     /// Unbounded extent: a Stream. The only inadmissible cardinality.\n30:     Stream,\n31: }\n32: \n33: impl Cardinality {\n34:     fn label(self) -> &'static str {\n35:         match self {\n36:             Cardinality::One => \"One\",\n37:             Cardinality::Opt => \"Opt\",\n38:             Cardinality::Fold => \"fold\",\n39:             Cardinality::Stream => \"Stream\",\n40:         }\n41:     }\n42: \n43:     /// Whether a sub-expression of this cardinality is admissible for offload. Only a\n44:     /// Stream is not: its extent is unknown, so it cannot be a kernel and must fall back\n45:     /// to scalar evaluation.\n46:     fn admissible(self) -> bool {\n47:         self != Cardinality::Stream\n48:     }\n49: }\n50: \n51: /// The cardinality an expression carries, from its type. A `Stream` is outermost and\n52: /// always wins when present; otherwise a `Vec` is a fold over known-extent data, an\n53: /// `Opt` is stream compaction, and anything else is a scalar `One`.\n54: fn cardinality(ty: &Type) -> Cardinality {\n55:     if matches!(ty, Type::Stream(_)) {\n56:         Cardinality::Stream\n57:     } else if ty.contains_vec() {\n58:         Cardinality::Fold\n59:     } else if ty.as_opt().is_some() {\n60:         Cardinality::Opt\n61:     } else {\n62:         Cardinality::One\n63:     }\n64: }\n65: \n66: /// The report: one line per sub-expression, in pre-order, across every function and the\n67: /// program body. Each line names the sub-expression, its cardinality, and whether that\n68: /// cardinality makes it an offloadable kernel or a scalar fallback.\n69: pub fn explain(program: &Program) -> String {\n70:     let mut out = String::new();\n71:     out.push_str(\"explain offload: vectorizability by cardinality\\n\");\n72:     for f in &program.funcs {\n73:         out.push_str(&format!(\"function {}:\\n\", f.name));\n74:         walk(&f.body, 1, &mut out);\n75:     }\n76:     out.push_str(\"program body:\\n\");\n77:     walk(&program.body, 1, &mut out);\n78:     out\n79: }\n80: \n81: fn walk(t: &Tir, depth: usize, out: &mut String) {\n82:     let card = cardinality(&t.ty);\n83:     let disposition = if card.admissible() {\n84:         \"offloadable kernel\"\n85:     } else {\n86:         \"scalar fallback\"\n87:     };\n88:     out.push_str(&format!(\n89:         \"{}{} : {} -- {} -- {}\\n\",\n90:         \"  \".repeat(depth),\n91:         kind_name(&t.kind),\n92:         t.ty,\n93:         card.label(),\n94:         disposition\n95:     ));\n96:     match &t.kind {\n97:         Kind::Str(_)\n98:         | Kind::Int(_)\n99:         | Kind::Var(_)\n100:         | Kind::Local(_)\n101:         | Kind::Input\n102:         | Kind::Inputs\n103:         | Kind::Lines => {}\n104:         Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| walk(i, depth + 1, out)),\n105:         Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n106:             fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| walk(v, depth + 1, out))\n107:         }\n108:         Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n109:             if let Some(p) = payload {\n110:                 walk(p, depth + 1, out);\n111:             }\n112:         }\n113:         Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n114:             if let Some(a) = arg {\n115:                 walk(a, depth + 1, out);\n116:             }\n117:         }\n118:         Kind::Concat(l, r)\n119:         | Kind::Compare { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n120:         | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n121:         | Kind::Arith { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n122:             walk(l, depth + 1, out);\n123:             walk(r, depth + 1, out);\n124:         }\n125:         Kind::Not(base) => walk(base, depth + 1, out),\n126:         Kind::Cond {\n127:             cond,\n128:             then,\n129:             otherwise,\n130:         } => {\n131:             walk(cond, depth + 1, out);\n132:             walk(then, depth + 1, out);\n133:             walk(otherwise, depth + 1, out);\n134:         }\n135:         Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n136:             walk(value, depth + 1, out);\n137:             walk(body, depth + 1, out);\n138:         }\n139:         Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n140:             walk(source, depth + 1, out);\n141:             walk(body, depth + 1, out);\n142:         }\n143:         Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n144:             walk(source, depth + 1, out);\n145:             walk(pred, depth + 1, out);\n146:         }\n147:         Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } => walk(base, depth + 1, out),\n148:         Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } => walk(arg, depth + 1, out),\n149:         Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n150:             walk(base, depth + 1, out);\n151:             walk(index, depth + 1, out);\n152:         }\n153:         Kind::Slice { base, start, end, .. } => {\n154:             walk(base, depth + 1, out);\n155:             if let Some(s) = start {\n156:                 walk(s, depth + 1, out);\n157:             }\n158:             if let Some(e) = end {\n159:                 walk(e, depth + 1, out);\n160:             }\n161:         }\n162:         Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n163:             walk(subject, depth + 1, out);\n164:             for arm in arms {\n165:                 if let Some(g) = &arm.guard {\n166:                     walk(g, depth + 1, out);\n167:                 }\n168:                 walk(&arm.body, depth + 1, out);\n169:             }\n170:         }\n171:     }\n172: }\n173: \n174: fn kind_name(k: &Kind) -> &'static str {\n175:     match k {\n176:         Kind::Str(_) => \"str\",\n177:         Kind::Int(_) => \"int\",\n178:         Kind::VecLit(_) => \"vec-literal\",\n179:         Kind::RecordLit { .. } => \"record-literal\",\n180:         Kind::EnumLit { .. } => \"enum-literal\",\n181:         Kind::OptMap { .. } => \"opt-map\",\n182:         Kind::Var(_) => \"var\",\n183:         Kind::Local(_) => \"local\",\n184:         Kind::Input => \"input\",\n185:         Kind::Inputs => \"inputs\",\n186:         Kind::Lines => \"lines\",\n187:         Kind::Call { .. } => \"call\",\n188:         Kind::Concat(..) => \"concat\",\n189:         Kind::Arith { .. } => \"arith\",\n190:         Kind::Cond { .. } => \"cond\",\n191:         Kind::Compare { .. } => \"compare\",\n192:         Kind::Logic { .. } => \"logic\",\n193:         Kind::Not(_) => \"not\",\n194:         Kind::Bind { .. } => \"bind\",\n195:         Kind::Map { .. } => \"map\",\n196:         Kind::Select { .. } => \"select\",\n197:         Kind::Field { .. } => \"field\",\n198:         Kind::Builtin { .. } => \"builtin\",\n199:         Kind::Unwrap { .. } => \"unwrap\",\n200:         Kind::Index { .. } => \"index\",\n201:         Kind::Slice { .. } => \"slice\",\n202:         Kind::Match { .. } => \"match\",\n203:     }\n204: }\n\n(End of file - total 204 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The `--explain-offload` diagnostic: which sub-expressions are offloadable and which\n//! fall back to scalar evaluation, and why.\n//!\n//! Vectorizability is silent and cardinality-derived (plans/questions.md Q8): a\n//! sub-expression is admissible for offload exactly when its cardinality is known --\n//! `One`, `Opt`, or a `fold` over a collection -- and not admissible when it is a\n//! `Stream`, whose extent is unbounded. The design's offload boundary and its layer\n//! boundary are the same boundary (draft.md, \"Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility\n//! predicate\"): everything in the value layer is a kernel candidate, and the effect\n//! layer is exactly what cannot be one.\n//!\n//! This is diagnostic-only. It computes nothing the backends do not already decide from\n//! the same types, and it changes neither the type system nor any signature. The flag\n//! just makes the otherwise-silent decision visible.\n\nuse crate::tir::{Kind, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::Type;\n\n/// The cardinality of an expression, read off its type. Admissibility is a property of\n/// the type, not of the operation (draft.md, \"The admissible input set\").","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src/offload.rs","text":"//! The `--explain-offload` diagnostic: which sub-expressions are offloadable and which\n//! fall back to scalar evaluation, and why.\n//!\n//! Vectorizability is silent and cardinality-derived (plans/questions.md Q8): a\n//! sub-expression is admissible for offload exactly when its cardinality is known --\n//! `One`, `Opt`, or a `fold` over a collection -- and not admissible when it is a\n//! `Stream`, whose extent is unbounded. The design's offload boundary and its layer\n//! boundary are the same boundary (draft.md, \"Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility\n//! predicate\"): everything in the value layer is a kernel candidate, and the effect\n//! layer is exactly what cannot be one.\n//!\n//! This is diagnostic-only. It computes nothing the backends do not already decide from\n//! the same types, and it changes neither the type system nor any signature. The flag\n//! just makes the otherwise-silent decision visible.\n\nuse crate::tir::{Kind, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::Type;\n\n/// The cardinality of an expression, read off its type. Admissibility is a property of\n/// the type, not of the operation (draft.md, \"The admissible input set\").\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\nenum Cardinality {\n    /// Exactly one value per input: a scalar.\n    One,\n    /// Zero or one: an Opt, admissible as stream compaction.\n    Opt,\n    /// A collection of known extent: a fold over a Vec.\n    Fold,\n    /// Unbounded extent: a Stream. The only inadmissible cardinality.\n    Stream,\n}\n\nimpl Cardinality {\n    fn label(self) -> &'static str {\n        match self {\n            Cardinality::One => \"One\",\n            Cardinality::Opt => \"Opt\",\n            Cardinality::Fold => \"fold\",\n            Cardinality::Stream => \"Stream\",\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether a sub-expression of this cardinality is admissible for offload. Only a\n    /// Stream is not: its extent is unknown, so it cannot be a kernel and must fall back\n    /// to scalar evaluation.\n    fn admissible(self) -> bool {\n        self != Cardinality::Stream\n    }\n}\n\n/// The cardinality an expression carries, from its type. A `Stream` is outermost and\n/// always wins when present; otherwise a `Vec` is a fold over known-extent data, an\n/// `Opt` is stream compaction, and anything else is a scalar `One`.\nfn cardinality(ty: &Type) -> Cardinality {\n    if matches!(ty, Type::Stream(_)) {\n        Cardinality::Stream\n    } else if ty.contains_vec() {\n        Cardinality::Fold\n    } else if ty.as_opt().is_some() {\n        Cardinality::Opt\n    } else {\n        Cardinality::One\n    }\n}\n\n/// The report: one line per sub-expression, in pre-order, across every function and the\n/// program body. Each line names the sub-expression, its cardinality, and whether that\n/// cardinality makes it an offloadable kernel or a scalar fallback.\npub fn explain(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    out.push_str(\"explain offload: vectorizability by cardinality\\n\");\n    for f in &program.funcs {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\"function {}:\\n\", f.name));\n        walk(&f.body, 1, &mut out);\n    }\n    out.push_str(\"program body:\\n\");\n    walk(&program.body, 1, &mut out);\n    out\n}\n\nfn walk(t: &Tir, depth: usize, out: &mut String) {\n    let card = cardinality(&t.ty);\n    let disposition = if card.admissible() {\n        \"offloadable kernel\"\n    } else {\n        \"scalar fallback\"\n    };\n    out.push_str(&format!(\n        \"{}{} : {} -- {} -- {}\\n\",\n        \"  \".repeat(depth),\n        kind_name(&t.kind),\n        t.ty,\n        card.label(),\n        disposition\n    ));\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Str(_)\n        | Kind::Int(_)\n        | Kind::Var(_)\n        | Kind::Local(_)\n        | Kind::Input\n        | Kind::Inputs\n        | Kind::Lines => {}\n        Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| walk(i, depth + 1, out)),\n        Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n            fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| walk(v, depth + 1, out))\n        }\n        Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n            if let Some(p) = payload {\n                walk(p, depth + 1, out);\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n            if let Some(a) = arg {\n                walk(a, depth + 1, out);\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Concat(l, r)\n        | Kind::Compare { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n        | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. }\n        | Kind::Arith { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n            walk(l, depth + 1, out);\n            walk(r, depth + 1, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Not(base) => walk(base, depth + 1, out),\n        Kind::Cond {\n            cond,\n            then,\n            otherwise,\n        } => {\n            walk(cond, depth + 1, out);\n            walk(then, depth + 1, out);\n            walk(otherwise, depth + 1, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n            walk(value, depth + 1, out);\n            walk(body, depth + 1, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n            walk(source, depth + 1, out);\n            walk(body, depth + 1, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n            walk(source, depth + 1, out);\n            walk(pred, depth + 1, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } => walk(base, depth + 1, out),\n        Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } => walk(arg, depth + 1, out),\n        Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n            walk(base, depth + 1, out);\n            walk(index, depth + 1, out);\n        }\n        Kind::Slice { base, start, end, .. } => {\n            walk(base, depth + 1, out);\n            if let Some(s) = start {\n                walk(s, depth + 1, out);\n            }\n            if let Some(e) = end {\n                walk(e, depth + 1, out);\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n            walk(subject, depth + 1, out);\n            for arm in arms {\n                if let Some(g) = &arm.guard {\n                    walk(g, depth + 1, out);\n                }\n                walk(&arm.body, depth + 1, out);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn kind_name(k: &Kind) -> &'static str {\n    match k {\n        Kind::Str(_) => \"str\",\n        Kind::Int(_) => \"int\",\n        Kind::VecLit(_) => \"vec-literal\",\n        Kind::RecordLit { .. } => \"record-literal\",\n        Kind::EnumLit { .. } => \"enum-literal\",\n        Kind::OptMap { .. } => \"opt-map\",\n        Kind::Var(_) => \"var\",\n        Kind::Local(_) => \"local\",\n        Kind::Input => \"input\",\n        Kind::Inputs => \"inputs\",\n        Kind::Lines => \"lines\",\n        Kind::Call { .. } => \"call\",\n        Kind::Concat(..) => \"concat\",\n        Kind::Arith { .. } => \"arith\",\n        Kind::Cond { .. } => \"cond\",\n        Kind::Compare { .. } => \"compare\",\n        Kind::Logic { .. } => \"logic\",\n        Kind::Not(_) => \"not\",\n        Kind::Bind { .. } => \"bind\",\n        Kind::Map { .. } => \"map\",\n        Kind::Select { .. } => \"select\",\n        Kind::Field { .. } => \"field\",\n        Kind::Builtin { .. } => \"builtin\",\n        Kind::Unwrap { .. } => \"unwrap\",\n        Kind::Index { .. } => \"index\",\n        Kind::Slice { .. } => \"slice\",\n        Kind::Match { .. } => \"match\",\n    }\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":204,"totalLines":204,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/offload.rs","time":{"start":1788285387553,"end":1788285387569}},"id":"prt_05e1d81ac001GlmywD1LsABl7A","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","messageID":"msg_05e1d58b4001gRiroWHTT9no1m"}}
