{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788285446539,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-93f4c47274d94c5b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src/tir.rs","limit":175,"offset":27},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src/tir.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n27: pub enum Kind {\n28:     Str(String),\n29:     Int(i64),\n30:     VecLit(Vec<Tir>),\n31:     /// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\n32:     /// its position in the type. That is what lets a backend address one by index rather than\n33:     /// searching for it.\n34:     RecordLit {\n35:         fields: Vec<(String, Tir)>,\n36:     },\n37:     /// A constructed enum value. The node's type is the enum, so a backend finds the variant's\n38:     /// position (its tag, where one is needed) and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\n39:     /// for that type's list rather than by carrying either here. `payload` is `None` for a unit\n40:     /// variant, which every backend renders as the bare variant-name string; a payload variant\n41:     /// is the single-key wrapper (ADR 0009).\n42:     EnumLit {\n43:         variant: String,\n44:         payload: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n45:     },\n46:     /// Rebuilds an Opt: present-preserving, absent-preserving. `body` runs with `param` bound\n47:     /// to the unwrapped payload only when `source` is present; an absent `source` passes\n48:     /// through untouched. Never surface syntax -- Opt has no general map or match a program can\n49:     /// reach (kantord/toylang#47's totality round owns that) -- this is only how the checker's\n50:     /// own reorder pass reaches inside an Opt payload (kantord/toylang#66): every other enum\n51:     /// reorders through `Match`/`EnumLit`, but Opt keeps a representation of its own in three\n52:     /// backends, so those two general nodes cannot reach it.\n53:     OptMap {\n54:         source: Box<Tir>,\n55:         param: LocalId,\n56:         body: Box<Tir>,\n57:     },\n58:     /// A name written in the source: today only a function parameter.\n59:     Var(String),\n60:     Local(LocalId),\n61:     /// The value read from stdin.\n62:     Input,\n63:     /// Every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line, eagerly collected into a `Vec<T>`.\n64:     Inputs,\n65:     /// The stream of lines read from stdin, read incrementally by whatever consumes it.\n66:     Lines,\n67:     Call {\n68:         func: String,\n69:         /// `None` for a call to a nullary function.\n70:         arg: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n71:     },\n72:     Concat(Box<Tir>, Box<Tir>),\n73:     /// Wrapping integer arithmetic, at the width the node's own type names: 32 bits for `Int`,\n74:     /// 64 for `Int64` (kantord/toylang#83) -- both operands always share it, since nothing\n75:     /// converts implicitly. Division and remainder stop the program on a zero divisor, which\n76:     /// is the only way arithmetic can fail.\n77:     Arith {\n78:         op: BinOp,\n79:         lhs: Box<Tir>,\n80:         rhs: Box<Tir>,\n81:     },\n82:     /// The condition is exactly one Bool, which is what turns jq's run-both-branches behaviour\n83:     /// into a type error here.\n84:     Cond {\n85:         cond: Box<Tir>,\n86:         then: Box<Tir>,\n87:         otherwise: Box<Tir>,\n88:     },\n89:     Compare {\n90:         op: BinOp,\n91:         lhs: Box<Tir>,\n92:         rhs: Box<Tir>,\n93:     },\n94:     /// `and` / `or` over two Bools, short-circuiting: `rhs` is evaluated only when `lhs` leaves\n95:     /// the answer open. That is not a nicety here -- division and `!` can stop the program, so\n96:     /// whether the right side runs is observable -- which is why every backend emits its own\n97:     /// short-circuiting operator rather than two evaluated operands combined afterwards.\n98:     Logic {\n99:         op: LogicOp,\n100:         lhs: Box<Tir>,\n101:         rhs: Box<Tir>,\n102:     },\n103:     /// `not b`.\n104:     Not(Box<Tir>),\n105:     /// `let local = value in body`, which is what `|` becomes once `.` has a name.\n106:     Bind {\n107:         local: LocalId,\n108:         value: Box<Tir>,\n109:         body: Box<Tir>,\n110:     },\n111:     /// Every element replaced by `body`, with `param` bound to each. Same loop as Select, kept\n112:     /// separate because the result's element type is the body's rather than the source's.\n113:     Map {\n114:         source: Box<Tir>,\n115:         param: LocalId,\n116:         body: Box<Tir>,\n117:     },\n118:     Select {\n119:         source: Box<Tir>,\n120:         param: LocalId,\n121:         pred: Box<Tir>,\n122:     },\n123:     /// Read `name` off `base`. How many Vec layers to descend through is `base.ty`'s doing and\n124:     /// is not stored, so it cannot disagree with the type.\n125:     Field {\n126:         base: Box<Tir>,\n127:         name: String,\n128:     },\n129:     /// Collapse one dimension of `base` at `index`, `depth` layers down.\n130:     ///\n131:     /// Unlike a field access this has to store its depth. A field access leaves a record behind,\n132:     /// so the depth is every Vec layer of the base; an index leaves a Vec behind, so the layers\n133:     /// below the one being collapsed are indistinguishable from the ones above it.\n134:     /// A unary builtin. Unary like every other function, so it needs no special call form.\n135:     Builtin {\n136:         which: Builtin,\n137:         arg: Box<Tir>,\n138:     },\n139:     /// Insist an Opt is present, `depth` layers down. Like a field access and unlike an index,\n140:     /// the depth is every Vec layer of the base, because an Opt is not a dimension.\n141:     Unwrap {\n142:         base: Box<Tir>,\n143:     },\n144:     Index {\n145:         base: Box<Tir>,\n146:         index: Box<Tir>,\n147:         depth: usize,\n148:         /// Whether an entry is a record, which decides if collapsing has to gather columns.\n149:         elem_is_record: bool,\n150:     },\n151:     /// Narrow a dimension to the `[start, end)` window, `depth` layers down, counting negative\n152:     /// bounds from the end. Out-of-range bounds clamp to the valid range rather than answering\n153:     /// `Opt` the way a collapsing `Index` does (kantord/toylang#143), jq's `.[a:b]`; `None`\n154:     /// means the dimension's own boundary (0 and its length), and `start >= end` after\n155:     /// clamping yields empty.\n156:     Slice {\n157:         base: Box<Tir>,\n158:         start: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n159:         end: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n160:         depth: usize,\n161:     },\n162:     /// First-match-wins dispatch over the subject: variant arms test its shape, guard arms\n163:     /// evaluate a Bool of their own. The checker has already resolved every name a pattern\n164:     /// bound, so an arm is only a test, a payload local to bind, and a body.\n165:     ///\n166:     /// When the chain is total (`partial` is false) -- every variant covered, or a default at\n167:     /// the end -- a backend may take the last arm without a test. A partial chain has no such\n168:     /// arm: every test is emitted, and falling off the end produces the absent `Opt`, which is\n169:     /// why the node's type is then `Opt` of the arms' common body type.\n170:     Match {\n171:         subject: Box<Tir>,\n172:         arms: Vec<MatchArm>,\n173:         partial: bool,\n174:     },\n175: }\n176: \n177: pub struct MatchArm {\n178:     /// `Some` tests the subject for this variant. `None` with a `guard` is a guard arm; `None`\n179:     /// with no guard is the default arm, which the checker keeps last.\n180:     pub variant: Option<String>,\n181:     /// The Bool deciding a guard arm, already checked in the subject's scope.\n182:     pub guard: Option<Tir>,\n183:     /// The local the payload binds to in a payload-variant arm; `.` and destructured field\n184:     /// names in the body both read through it.\n185:     pub payload: Option<LocalId>,\n186:     pub body: Tir,\n187: }\n188: \n189: /// The functions the language provides. Each is unary, and so is every user function: something\n190: /// wanting two arguments takes a record, which is what a record literal is for in argument\n191: /// position.\n192: #[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n193: pub enum Builtin {\n194:     /// `str(n)`, rendering an Int the way the printer does but reachable from a program.\n195:     IntToStr,\n196:     /// `i64(n)`, `Int -> Int64`: the one bridge between the two integer types, explicit\n197:     /// because nothing widens implicitly (kantord/toylang#83). A no-op on every backend whose\n198:     /// runtime integers are already 64 bits wide; JS builds a BigInt, and Go and Rust spell\n199:     /// the cast.\n200:     IntToI64,\n201:     /// `range(n)`, the integers from zero up to but not including n. Zero-based, matching jq,\n\n(Showing lines 27-201 of 606. Use offset=202 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"pub enum Kind {\n    Str(String),\n    Int(i64),\n    VecLit(Vec<Tir>),\n    /// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\n    /// its position in the type. That is what lets a backend address one by index rather than\n    /// searching for it.\n    RecordLit {\n        fields: Vec<(String, Tir)>,\n    },\n    /// A constructed enum value. The node's type is the enum, so a backend finds the variant's\n    /// position (its tag, where one is needed) and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\n    /// for that type's list rather than by carrying either here. `payload` is `None` for a unit\n    /// variant, which every backend renders as the bare variant-name string; a payload variant\n    /// is the single-key wrapper (ADR 0009).\n    EnumLit {\n        variant: String,\n        payload: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n    },\n    /// Rebuilds an Opt: present-preserving, absent-preserving. `body` runs with `param` bound","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/src/tir.rs","text":"pub enum Kind {\n    Str(String),\n    Int(i64),\n    VecLit(Vec<Tir>),\n    /// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\n    /// its position in the type. That is what lets a backend address one by index rather than\n    /// searching for it.\n    RecordLit {\n        fields: Vec<(String, Tir)>,\n    },\n    /// A constructed enum value. The node's type is the enum, so a backend finds the variant's\n    /// position (its tag, where one is needed) and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\n    /// for that type's list rather than by carrying either here. `payload` is `None` for a unit\n    /// variant, which every backend renders as the bare variant-name string; a payload variant\n    /// is the single-key wrapper (ADR 0009).\n    EnumLit {\n        variant: String,\n        payload: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n    },\n    /// Rebuilds an Opt: present-preserving, absent-preserving. `body` runs with `param` bound\n    /// to the unwrapped payload only when `source` is present; an absent `source` passes\n    /// through untouched. Never surface syntax -- Opt has no general map or match a program can\n    /// reach (kantord/toylang#47's totality round owns that) -- this is only how the checker's\n    /// own reorder pass reaches inside an Opt payload (kantord/toylang#66): every other enum\n    /// reorders through `Match`/`EnumLit`, but Opt keeps a representation of its own in three\n    /// backends, so those two general nodes cannot reach it.\n    OptMap {\n        source: Box<Tir>,\n        param: LocalId,\n        body: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// A name written in the source: today only a function parameter.\n    Var(String),\n    Local(LocalId),\n    /// The value read from stdin.\n    Input,\n    /// Every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line, eagerly collected into a `Vec<T>`.\n    Inputs,\n    /// The stream of lines read from stdin, read incrementally by whatever consumes it.\n    Lines,\n    Call {\n        func: String,\n        /// `None` for a call to a nullary function.\n        arg: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n    },\n    Concat(Box<Tir>, Box<Tir>),\n    /// Wrapping integer arithmetic, at the width the node's own type names: 32 bits for `Int`,\n    /// 64 for `Int64` (kantord/toylang#83) -- both operands always share it, since nothing\n    /// converts implicitly. Division and remainder stop the program on a zero divisor, which\n    /// is the only way arithmetic can fail.\n    Arith {\n        op: BinOp,\n        lhs: Box<Tir>,\n        rhs: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// The condition is exactly one Bool, which is what turns jq's run-both-branches behaviour\n    /// into a type error here.\n    Cond {\n        cond: Box<Tir>,\n        then: Box<Tir>,\n        otherwise: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    Compare {\n        op: BinOp,\n        lhs: Box<Tir>,\n        rhs: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// `and` / `or` over two Bools, short-circuiting: `rhs` is evaluated only when `lhs` leaves\n    /// the answer open. That is not a nicety here -- division and `!` can stop the program, so\n    /// whether the right side runs is observable -- which is why every backend emits its own\n    /// short-circuiting operator rather than two evaluated operands combined afterwards.\n    Logic {\n        op: LogicOp,\n        lhs: Box<Tir>,\n        rhs: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// `not b`.\n    Not(Box<Tir>),\n    /// `let local = value in body`, which is what `|` becomes once `.` has a name.\n    Bind {\n        local: LocalId,\n        value: Box<Tir>,\n        body: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// Every element replaced by `body`, with `param` bound to each. Same loop as Select, kept\n    /// separate because the result's element type is the body's rather than the source's.\n    Map {\n        source: Box<Tir>,\n        param: LocalId,\n        body: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    Select {\n        source: Box<Tir>,\n        param: LocalId,\n        pred: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// Read `name` off `base`. How many Vec layers to descend through is `base.ty`'s doing and\n    /// is not stored, so it cannot disagree with the type.\n    Field {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        name: String,\n    },\n    /// Collapse one dimension of `base` at `index`, `depth` layers down.\n    ///\n    /// Unlike a field access this has to store its depth. A field access leaves a record behind,\n    /// so the depth is every Vec layer of the base; an index leaves a Vec behind, so the layers\n    /// below the one being collapsed are indistinguishable from the ones above it.\n    /// A unary builtin. Unary like every other function, so it needs no special call form.\n    Builtin {\n        which: Builtin,\n        arg: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// Insist an Opt is present, `depth` layers down. Like a field access and unlike an index,\n    /// the depth is every Vec layer of the base, because an Opt is not a dimension.\n    Unwrap {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    Index {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        index: Box<Tir>,\n        depth: usize,\n        /// Whether an entry is a record, which decides if collapsing has to gather columns.\n        elem_is_record: bool,\n    },\n    /// Narrow a dimension to the `[start, end)` window, `depth` layers down, counting negative\n    /// bounds from the end. Out-of-range bounds clamp to the valid range rather than answering\n    /// `Opt` the way a collapsing `Index` does (kantord/toylang#143), jq's `.[a:b]`; `None`\n    /// means the dimension's own boundary (0 and its length), and `start >= end` after\n    /// clamping yields empty.\n    Slice {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        start: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n        end: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n        depth: usize,\n    },\n    /// First-match-wins dispatch over the subject: variant arms test its shape, guard arms\n    /// evaluate a Bool of their own. The checker has already resolved every name a pattern\n    /// bound, so an arm is only a test, a payload local to bind, and a body.\n    ///\n    /// When the chain is total (`partial` is false) -- every variant covered, or a default at\n    /// the end -- a backend may take the last arm without a test. A partial chain has no such\n    /// arm: every test is emitted, and falling off the end produces the absent `Opt`, which is\n    /// why the node's type is then `Opt` of the arms' common body type.\n    Match {\n        subject: Box<Tir>,\n        arms: Vec<MatchArm>,\n        partial: bool,\n    },\n}\n\npub struct MatchArm {\n    /// `Some` tests the subject for this variant. `None` with a `guard` is a guard arm; `None`\n    /// with no guard is the default arm, which the checker keeps last.\n    pub variant: Option<String>,\n    /// The Bool deciding a guard arm, already checked in the subject's scope.\n    pub guard: Option<Tir>,\n    /// The local the payload binds to in a payload-variant arm; `.` and destructured field\n    /// names in the body both read through it.\n    pub payload: Option<LocalId>,\n    pub body: Tir,\n}\n\n/// The functions the language provides. Each is unary, and so is every user function: something\n/// wanting two arguments takes a record, which is what a record literal is for in argument\n/// position.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Builtin {\n    /// `str(n)`, rendering an Int the way the printer does but reachable from a program.\n    IntToStr,\n    /// `i64(n)`, `Int -> Int64`: the one bridge between the two integer types, explicit\n    /// because nothing widens implicitly (kantord/toylang#83). A no-op on every backend whose\n    /// runtime integers are already 64 bits wide; JS builds a BigInt, and Go and Rust spell\n    /// the cast.\n    IntToI64,\n    /// `range(n)`, the integers from zero up to but not including n. Zero-based, matching jq,","lineStart":27,"lineEnd":201,"totalLines":606,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/tir.rs","time":{"start":1788285446534,"end":1788285446538}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The `Program` struct matches. Now let me try to compile with `just check`. But first let me review the code for correctness against the actual Kind variants. Let me check the full `Kind` enum to make sure offload.rs's match arms are exhaustive/correct, and whether there are Kind variants not covered.\n\nLet me look at the Kind enum fully.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e1e6509001fCsldX8sx7PKEn","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","messageID":"msg_05e1e5bec001mz9H8BQg4aujtx"}}
