{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788285522589,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01a05e1f8ea67c91892fd30e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -l \"Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility\" --glob '*.md' . | head"},"output":"./draft.md\n","metadata":{"output":"./draft.md\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -l \"Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility\" --glob '*.md' . | head","time":{"start":1788285522582,"end":1788285522587}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_05e1f8f31001fHAtn3OJ0vA1dk","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","messageID":"msg_05e1f8aaa001Zqhqj7U7fpFvnq"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788285523885,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a05e1f92167c808d5d9a59","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/plans/questions.md","offset":157,"limit":100},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/plans/questions.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n157: ### Q8. Is vectorizability visible in the type system, or a silent optimization?\n158: \n159: Reporting it\n160: means a second effect alongside cardinality, and a visible fast-path/slow-path distinction in\n161: signatures. Hiding it makes performance unpredictable in exactly the way this design is\n162: trying to avoid. Note the two effects are orthogonal: `select` changes cardinality and\n163: vectorizes fine as a mask, while `first` changes cardinality the same way and cannot\n164: vectorize at all.\n165: \n166: ### Q9. Are vectors multidimensional, with `[]` as projection?\n167: \n168: See the TODO and response in the\n169: cardinality section. Unifies indexing with iteration, but disturbs the claim that there are\n170: exactly two layer shifters, and per-dimension cardinality only describes rectangular data\n171: while JSON is ragged.\n172: \n173: ### Q10. Is uniqueness analysis in scope, for deciding when a lens materializes?\n174: \n175: Deciding when a projection lens can materialize instead\n176:  of staying a view requires knowing no other reference to the source survives. That is\n177:  linearity or uniqueness typing, the machinery deliberately avoided in [the ordering question](#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams).\n178: \n179: ### Q11. How does the query/transformation split manifest in the type system?\n180: \n181: SETTLED: it does not need to. `map` and `select` are the same operation with the multiplicity\n182: stored in different places, so the split never reaches the type system; see [the two-layer\n183: section](../draft.md#the-core-idea-two-layers).\n184: \n185: ### Q12. On a type mismatch, does field access error, yield null, or something third?\n186: \n187: SETTLED: something third. Field access desugars to a lens with three distinguishable outcomes\n188: -- a value, a specific absence, and a specific error; see [the field-access\n189: section](../draft.md#field-access-is-a-lens).\n190: \n191: ### Q13. Does the layer shift run only one way, with no value-to-effect operator?\n192: \n193: If effect multiplicity is born only from\n194:  streaming sources and dies only into values through `[...]`, then no value-to-effect\n195:  operator is needed, because degrading a `Vec` forgets its extent and buys nothing. LEANING\n196:  toward yes. This decides [the streams question](#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) with it, since the only thing that would break it is a value\n197:  with genuinely unknown extent, which is what a first-class stream value would be. The streams\n198:  question is now settled the compatible way, and this lifecycle -- born at `inputs`/`lines`,\n199:  dead at `collect` or a sink -- became the `Stream<T>` typing rule, so reversing this lean now\n200:  means amending that decision too.\n201: \n202: ### Q14. Does `select` return a masked view, a selection vector, or a copy?\n203: \n204: See the section on\n205:  whether a value-layer `select` copies. A bitmask breaks `Vec`'s constant-time indexing\n206:  promise, a selection vector keeps it and pays memory per survivor, and either view pins its\n207:  whole source buffer alive.\n208: \n209: ### Q15. Backend: LLVM via inkwell, Cranelift, or both?\n210: \n211: SETTLED: LLVM via inkwell, built and running. Recorded in\n212: [ADR 0005](../docs/adr/0005-llvm-via-inkwell-for-the-native-backend.md).\n213: \n214: ### Q16. String representation, given WTF-16 on the JS target\n215: \n216: The three\n217:  options are WTF-16 everywhere, UTF-8 everywhere with the JavaScript-shaped API emulated, or\n218:  designing the difference away by never exposing code-unit indexing or length. Only the third\n219:  is cheap on both sides. It has to be decided early because it constrains the string API\n220:  permanently.\n221: \n222: ### Q17. Is there a dense tensor type, constructed explicitly?\n223: \n224: `@f32` as a narrowing constructor\n225:  that hard-fails rather than an inference, with `reshape` attaching shape. It is also the\n226:  second number type, a deliberate lossy exit from the `f64` commitment.\n227: \n228: ### Q18. Does `.[]` on a rank-2 tensor yield rows or scalars?\n229: \n230: NumPy and APL both yield rows,\n231:  which makes `map` rank-polymorphic and gives row sums as `map(fold(add; 0))` with no new\n232:  syntax. Then rank-1 yields scalars and full linearization needs a separate flattening view.\n233: \n234: ### Q19. How are nulls carried in a dense typed buffer?\n235: \n236: JSON has null and an `f32` buffer does\n237:  not. NaN as a sentinel collides with genuine NaN. Arrow's separate validity bitmask solves\n238:  it and brings zero-copy interop with Polars, DuckDB and pandas.\n239: \n240: ### Q20. How are blocking operators (`sort`, `group_by`, joins) classified?\n241: \n242: `sort`, `group_by` and joins are one value in and\n243:  one value out, so the per-element cardinality mapping does not describe them. They need the\n244:  whole input before producing anything and are parallelizable by other means. The\n245:  kernel-admissibility result covers elementwise filters only, and this is the gap it leaves.\n246: \n247: ### Q21. What guarantees batch size is unobservable over a batched stream?\n248: \n249: Argued in [the admissible input set, and where batching comes from](../draft.md#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from) rather than\n250: here, since it arrived with that material. The leaning is the trait law that operations commute\n251: with reification, which is what makes a batch boundary invisible to a program.\n252: \n253: ### Q22. Are dense and masked vectors distinguishable in the type?\n254: \n255: Argued in [the admissible input set, and where batching comes from](../draft.md#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), where it\n256: appears as the observation that a masked view and a dense buffer have different launch\n\n(Showing lines 157-256 of 553. Use offset=257 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"### Q8. Is vectorizability visible in the type system, or a silent optimization?\n\nReporting it\nmeans a second effect alongside cardinality, and a visible fast-path/slow-path distinction in\nsignatures. Hiding it makes performance unpredictable in exactly the way this design is\ntrying to avoid. Note the two effects are orthogonal: `select` changes cardinality and\nvectorizes fine as a mask, while `first` changes cardinality the same way and cannot\nvectorize at all.\n\n### Q9. Are vectors multidimensional, with `[]` as projection?\n\nSee the TODO and response in the\ncardinality section. Unifies indexing with iteration, but disturbs the claim that there are\nexactly two layer shifters, and per-dimension cardinality only describes rectangular data\nwhile JSON is ragged.\n\n### Q10. Is uniqueness analysis in scope, for deciding when a lens materializes?\n\nDeciding when a projection lens can materialize instead\n of staying a view requires knowing no other reference to the source survives. That is","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/plans/questions.md","text":"### Q8. Is vectorizability visible in the type system, or a silent optimization?\n\nReporting it\nmeans a second effect alongside cardinality, and a visible fast-path/slow-path distinction in\nsignatures. Hiding it makes performance unpredictable in exactly the way this design is\ntrying to avoid. Note the two effects are orthogonal: `select` changes cardinality and\nvectorizes fine as a mask, while `first` changes cardinality the same way and cannot\nvectorize at all.\n\n### Q9. Are vectors multidimensional, with `[]` as projection?\n\nSee the TODO and response in the\ncardinality section. Unifies indexing with iteration, but disturbs the claim that there are\nexactly two layer shifters, and per-dimension cardinality only describes rectangular data\nwhile JSON is ragged.\n\n### Q10. Is uniqueness analysis in scope, for deciding when a lens materializes?\n\nDeciding when a projection lens can materialize instead\n of staying a view requires knowing no other reference to the source survives. That is\n linearity or uniqueness typing, the machinery deliberately avoided in [the ordering question](#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams).\n\n### Q11. How does the query/transformation split manifest in the type system?\n\nSETTLED: it does not need to. `map` and `select` are the same operation with the multiplicity\nstored in different places, so the split never reaches the type system; see [the two-layer\nsection](../draft.md#the-core-idea-two-layers).\n\n### Q12. On a type mismatch, does field access error, yield null, or something third?\n\nSETTLED: something third. Field access desugars to a lens with three distinguishable outcomes\n-- a value, a specific absence, and a specific error; see [the field-access\nsection](../draft.md#field-access-is-a-lens).\n\n### Q13. Does the layer shift run only one way, with no value-to-effect operator?\n\nIf effect multiplicity is born only from\n streaming sources and dies only into values through `[...]`, then no value-to-effect\n operator is needed, because degrading a `Vec` forgets its extent and buys nothing. LEANING\n toward yes. This decides [the streams question](#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) with it, since the only thing that would break it is a value\n with genuinely unknown extent, which is what a first-class stream value would be. The streams\n question is now settled the compatible way, and this lifecycle -- born at `inputs`/`lines`,\n dead at `collect` or a sink -- became the `Stream<T>` typing rule, so reversing this lean now\n means amending that decision too.\n\n### Q14. Does `select` return a masked view, a selection vector, or a copy?\n\nSee the section on\n whether a value-layer `select` copies. A bitmask breaks `Vec`'s constant-time indexing\n promise, a selection vector keeps it and pays memory per survivor, and either view pins its\n whole source buffer alive.\n\n### Q15. Backend: LLVM via inkwell, Cranelift, or both?\n\nSETTLED: LLVM via inkwell, built and running. Recorded in\n[ADR 0005](../docs/adr/0005-llvm-via-inkwell-for-the-native-backend.md).\n\n### Q16. String representation, given WTF-16 on the JS target\n\nThe three\n options are WTF-16 everywhere, UTF-8 everywhere with the JavaScript-shaped API emulated, or\n designing the difference away by never exposing code-unit indexing or length. Only the third\n is cheap on both sides. It has to be decided early because it constrains the string API\n permanently.\n\n### Q17. Is there a dense tensor type, constructed explicitly?\n\n`@f32` as a narrowing constructor\n that hard-fails rather than an inference, with `reshape` attaching shape. It is also the\n second number type, a deliberate lossy exit from the `f64` commitment.\n\n### Q18. Does `.[]` on a rank-2 tensor yield rows or scalars?\n\nNumPy and APL both yield rows,\n which makes `map` rank-polymorphic and gives row sums as `map(fold(add; 0))` with no new\n syntax. Then rank-1 yields scalars and full linearization needs a separate flattening view.\n\n### Q19. How are nulls carried in a dense typed buffer?\n\nJSON has null and an `f32` buffer does\n not. NaN as a sentinel collides with genuine NaN. Arrow's separate validity bitmask solves\n it and brings zero-copy interop with Polars, DuckDB and pandas.\n\n### Q20. How are blocking operators (`sort`, `group_by`, joins) classified?\n\n`sort`, `group_by` and joins are one value in and\n one value out, so the per-element cardinality mapping does not describe them. They need the\n whole input before producing anything and are parallelizable by other means. The\n kernel-admissibility result covers elementwise filters only, and this is the gap it leaves.\n\n### Q21. What guarantees batch size is unobservable over a batched stream?\n\nArgued in [the admissible input set, and where batching comes from](../draft.md#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from) rather than\nhere, since it arrived with that material. The leaning is the trait law that operations commute\nwith reification, which is what makes a batch boundary invisible to a program.\n\n### Q22. Are dense and masked vectors distinguishable in the type?\n\nArgued in [the admissible input set, and where batching comes from](../draft.md#the-admissible-input-set-and-where-batching-comes-from), where it\nappears as the observation that a masked view and a dense buffer have different launch","lineStart":157,"lineEnd":256,"totalLines":553,"truncated":true}},"title":"plans/questions.md","time":{"start":1788285523875,"end":1788285523883}},"id":"prt_05e1f92a2001sx5gTpdicgORSV","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","messageID":"msg_05e1f8aaa001Zqhqj7U7fpFvnq"}}
