{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788285533666,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"id":"prt_05e1fbddd001laLtSD6mmZeDjK","messageID":"msg_05e1fb58c001olKUKcU6CUcNaZ","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","snapshot":"6d47c855c38b081abd80c6dced2937871ad07835","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788285536668,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a0df718433beb660","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/draft.md","limit":60,"offset":1000},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1000: computes, and on a backend with no device it lowers to the ordinary loop. That keeps the earlier\n1001: result intact: choosing between kernel and vector loop stays a late decision.\n1002: \n1003: ### The same predicate governs CPU vectorization\n1004: \n1005: LLVM's loop vectorizer rewrites a scalar loop to process several elements per iteration with no\n1006: annotation, and its SLP vectorizer does the same for repeated straight-line operations. It is\n1007: automatic, with three qualifiers: it runs only at `-O2` or above, so the pass pipeline has to be\n1008: run rather than merely emitting IR; it has to prove legality; and it has to judge the result\n1009: profitable against a target cost model.\n1010: \n1011: The legality blockers, in roughly the order they bite in practice, are aliasing, loop-carried\n1012: dependencies, floating-point reductions, calls in the loop body, unknown trip counts combined\n1013: with early exits, and non-unit strides.\n1014: \n1015: Every one of those is something this design can guarantee away statically:\n1016: \n1017: | blocker | what removes it |\n1018: |---|---|\n1019: | aliasing, the most common cause | immutable inputs and a distinct output buffer, so `noalias` is emitable and the runtime overlap check disappears |\n1020: | loop-carried dependency | a pure elementwise filter captures no mutable state |\n1021: | float reduction | `fold` declares associativity, so `reassoc` is legitimate; `reduce` does not, so it is not |\n1022: | calls in the body | a fused pipeline is one loop body with everything inlined |\n1023: | unknown or non-unit stride | a dense buffer has unit stride known at compile time |\n1024: \n1025: So the offload check and the \"will this vectorize\" check are nearly the same predicate. That is\n1026: the good outcome: a region either dispatches to a kernel or lowers to a loop that reliably hits\n1027: NEON or AVX-512, and choosing between them is a late decision about *where to run* rather than a\n1028: semantic fork.\n1029: \n1030: Worth building in from the start: verify rather than hope. `-Rpass=loop-vectorize` and\n1031: `-Rpass-missed=loop-vectorize` report which loops vectorized and why the others did not, and a\n1032: regression there should fail a test rather than quietly cost throughput.\n1033: \n1034: ### Backend choice\n1035: \n1036: Not a question about JSON-shaped types. Both LLVM and Cranelift bottom out in integers, floats,\n1037: vectors, pointers, and memory; neither has a string, a collector, or a number tower. Those\n1038: semantics live in the front end and the runtime, and the backend never sees a string at all,\n1039: only a pointer and a length. If semantics drift across targets it is because they were defined\n1040: in the lowering rather than above it.\n1041: \n1042: What does differ:\n1043: \n1044: ```\n1045:                      LLVM (via inkwell)              Cranelift\n1046: SIMD                 vector + scalable vector types  128-bit vector types, well tested\n1047: auto-vectorization   loop and SLP vectorizers        NONE, explicit SIMD in and out\n1048: GPU                  NVPTX, AMDGPU, SPIR-V, raw IR   none, and not on the roadmap\n1049: wasm output          wasm32 target                   none; Cranelift consumes wasm\n1050: build                pinned system LLVM, C++ chain   pure cargo\n1051: compile speed        slower                          5 to 10 times faster\n1052: ```\n1053: \n1054: Two things follow. Cranelift never vectorizes for you, which matters a great deal here, because\n1055: the whole argument above is that this design can hand a vectorizer exactly the loops it likes.\n1056: And if the browser story ever becomes WebAssembly rather than emitted JavaScript source, LLVM\n1057: runs the *same* IR through one pipeline for both native and web, which is the strongest available\n1058: guarantee that semantics do not vary by platform. Cranelift produces nothing for the web, so that\n1059: would need a second unrelated backend and the drift risk would be entirely self-policed.\n\n(Showing lines 1000-1059 of 2480. Use offset=1060 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"computes, and on a backend with no device it lowers to the ordinary loop. That keeps the earlier\nresult intact: choosing between kernel and vector loop stays a late decision.\n\n### The same predicate governs CPU vectorization\n\nLLVM's loop vectorizer rewrites a scalar loop to process several elements per iteration with no\nannotation, and its SLP vectorizer does the same for repeated straight-line operations. It is\nautomatic, with three qualifiers: it runs only at `-O2` or above, so the pass pipeline has to be\nrun rather than merely emitting IR; it has to prove legality; and it has to judge the result\nprofitable against a target cost model.\n\nThe legality blockers, in roughly the order they bite in practice, are aliasing, loop-carried\ndependencies, floating-point reductions, calls in the loop body, unknown trip counts combined\nwith early exits, and non-unit strides.\n\nEvery one of those is something this design can guarantee away statically:\n\n| blocker | what removes it |\n|---|---|\n| aliasing, the most common cause | immutable inputs and a distinct output buffer, so `noalias` is emitable and the runtime overlap check disappears |","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/draft.md","text":"computes, and on a backend with no device it lowers to the ordinary loop. That keeps the earlier\nresult intact: choosing between kernel and vector loop stays a late decision.\n\n### The same predicate governs CPU vectorization\n\nLLVM's loop vectorizer rewrites a scalar loop to process several elements per iteration with no\nannotation, and its SLP vectorizer does the same for repeated straight-line operations. It is\nautomatic, with three qualifiers: it runs only at `-O2` or above, so the pass pipeline has to be\nrun rather than merely emitting IR; it has to prove legality; and it has to judge the result\nprofitable against a target cost model.\n\nThe legality blockers, in roughly the order they bite in practice, are aliasing, loop-carried\ndependencies, floating-point reductions, calls in the loop body, unknown trip counts combined\nwith early exits, and non-unit strides.\n\nEvery one of those is something this design can guarantee away statically:\n\n| blocker | what removes it |\n|---|---|\n| aliasing, the most common cause | immutable inputs and a distinct output buffer, so `noalias` is emitable and the runtime overlap check disappears |\n| loop-carried dependency | a pure elementwise filter captures no mutable state |\n| float reduction | `fold` declares associativity, so `reassoc` is legitimate; `reduce` does not, so it is not |\n| calls in the body | a fused pipeline is one loop body with everything inlined |\n| unknown or non-unit stride | a dense buffer has unit stride known at compile time |\n\nSo the offload check and the \"will this vectorize\" check are nearly the same predicate. That is\nthe good outcome: a region either dispatches to a kernel or lowers to a loop that reliably hits\nNEON or AVX-512, and choosing between them is a late decision about *where to run* rather than a\nsemantic fork.\n\nWorth building in from the start: verify rather than hope. `-Rpass=loop-vectorize` and\n`-Rpass-missed=loop-vectorize` report which loops vectorized and why the others did not, and a\nregression there should fail a test rather than quietly cost throughput.\n\n### Backend choice\n\nNot a question about JSON-shaped types. Both LLVM and Cranelift bottom out in integers, floats,\nvectors, pointers, and memory; neither has a string, a collector, or a number tower. Those\nsemantics live in the front end and the runtime, and the backend never sees a string at all,\nonly a pointer and a length. If semantics drift across targets it is because they were defined\nin the lowering rather than above it.\n\nWhat does differ:\n\n```\n                     LLVM (via inkwell)              Cranelift\nSIMD                 vector + scalable vector types  128-bit vector types, well tested\nauto-vectorization   loop and SLP vectorizers        NONE, explicit SIMD in and out\nGPU                  NVPTX, AMDGPU, SPIR-V, raw IR   none, and not on the roadmap\nwasm output          wasm32 target                   none; Cranelift consumes wasm\nbuild                pinned system LLVM, C++ chain   pure cargo\ncompile speed        slower                          5 to 10 times faster\n```\n\nTwo things follow. Cranelift never vectorizes for you, which matters a great deal here, because\nthe whole argument above is that this design can hand a vectorizer exactly the loops it likes.\nAnd if the browser story ever becomes WebAssembly rather than emitted JavaScript source, LLVM\nruns the *same* IR through one pipeline for both native and web, which is the strongest available\nguarantee that semantics do not vary by platform. Cranelift produces nothing for the web, so that\nwould need a second unrelated backend and the drift risk would be entirely self-policed.","lineStart":1000,"lineEnd":1059,"totalLines":2480,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788285536654,"end":1788285536667}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me read a bit more of the admissible input set section, and then the section around line 1025 \"So the offload check and the 'will this vectorize' check are nearly the same predicate.\"","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e1fbf77001oaCaNzc82OEr5e","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","messageID":"msg_05e1fb58c001olKUKcU6CUcNaZ"}}
