{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788285578757,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a0de1e6adc4f2e67","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls tests/"},"output":"aliases.rs\nbackend_go.rs\nbackend_jq.rs\nbackend_js.rs\nbackend_llvm.rs\nbackend_py.rs\nbackend_rust.rs\nbare_application.rs\nchar.rs\ncorpus\ncorpus.rs\ndocs.rs\nenums.rs\neuler_real_data.rs\nexport_site.rs\nfmt_examples.rs\nfmt_project.rs\nfmt.rs\ngenerics.rs\nint64.rs\njsonlines.rs\nmatcher.rs\nopt.rs\nreductions.rs\nsnapshots\nstep_1.rs\nstep_2.rs\nstep_3.rs\nstep_4.rs\nstep_5.rs\nstreaming.rs\nstreams.rs\nsupport\ntag_corpus.rs\ntype_flow.rs\nunused.rs\nunwrap.rs\n","metadata":{"output":"aliases.rs\nbackend_go.rs\nbackend_jq.rs\nbackend_js.rs\nbackend_llvm.rs\nbackend_py.rs\nbackend_rust.rs\nbare_application.rs\nchar.rs\ncorpus\ncorpus.rs\ndocs.rs\nenums.rs\neuler_real_data.rs\nexport_site.rs\nfmt_examples.rs\nfmt_project.rs\nfmt.rs\ngenerics.rs\nint64.rs\njsonlines.rs\nmatcher.rs\nopt.rs\nreductions.rs\nsnapshots\nstep_1.rs\nstep_2.rs\nstep_3.rs\nstep_4.rs\nstep_5.rs\nstreaming.rs\nstreams.rs\nsupport\ntag_corpus.rs\ntype_flow.rs\nunused.rs\nunwrap.rs\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls tests/","time":{"start":1788285578752,"end":1788285578755}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There's a subtlety in the cardinality function. `contains_vec` returns true for `Type::Stream(_)` too. But the cardinality function checks `Stream` first, so a `Stream` type would be caught by the first branch. Good. But there's an issue: `contains_vec` returns true if a Vec appears ANYWHERE in the type (e.g., a Record containing a Vec field). The cardinality function checks `contains_vec` which would make a record with a vec field a \"Fold\" cardinality. Is that right?\n\nThe design says \"Admissibility is a property of the type, not of the operation\" and \"admissible = scalars, and anything of known cardinality; one pre-allocated buffer plus a little lens metadata\". A record containing a Vec field has known cardinality (it's a collection of records). So labeling it \"fold\" seems roughly fine. But actually the cardinality being per the outer structure...\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider. The `as_opt` check returns Some only when the top-level type IS Opt. And `contains_vec` checks if a Vec appears anywhere. This is slightly inconsistent - `as_opt` is top-level only but `contains_vec` is anywhere. For a type `Vec<Opt<Int>>`, contains_vec is true (Fold), as_opt is None. For `Opt<Vec<Int>>`, as_opt is Some (Opt), but contains_vec also true. The order is: Stream, then contains_vec (Fold), then as_opt (Opt). So `Opt<Vec<Int>>` would be classified as Fold (because contains_vec is true and checked before as_opt). Hmm.\n\nThis is getting into design minutiae. The core requirement of the issue is satisfied - the flag annotates which sub-expressions are offloadable kernels and which fall back, by cardinality. The exact granularity of the cardinality mapping is a design decision.\n\nGiven the round-2 ruling is about cardinality-derived vectorizability, the mapping from the draft is:\n```\nOne<T>     -> elementwise map kernel\nOpt<T>     -> stream compaction\nfold       -> reduction\nStream<T>  -> NOT admissible\n```\n\nThe current implementation is reasonable. Let me not overthink this. But let me reconsider whether the current implementation truly represents \"which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which fell back\". \n\nActually, I want to reconsider the design of what \"explain\" should show. The issue says \"annotates compiled output with which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which fell back, and why\". The \"why\" is the cardinality. The current implementation shows cardinality + disposition + type. That's a reasonable annotation.\n\nLet me now think about whether this needs tests. The definition of done: \"implementation complete; `just check` green; `just fmt` and `just clippy` clean on code you touched\".\n\nAGENTS.md tests section: \"Every fixed bug gets the test that would have caught it.\" and the corpus discussion. But this is a new feature, not a bug fix. Should I add a test for the explain output? \n\nThe issue is about a diagnostic. Let me look at how tests are structured in this repo - there are test files like tests/step_1.rs, etc. Let me check if there's a natural place to test the offload explain. \n\nActually, given the definition of done focuses on `just check` green + fmt + clippy, and the feature is a diagnostic, I think adding a small test would be good practice but let me check the test conventions. Let me look at how the tests are organized and whether adding a test is warranted.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The feature is an opt-in diagnostic. Let me check if there's an existing pattern. Let me look at the tests directory structure and how a similar diagnostic feature was tested.\n\nActually, let me focus. The definition of done is clear. Let me:\n1. Run `just fmt` on the code I touched.\n2. Run `just clippy`.\n3. Commit.\n\nBut wait - should I add a test? Let me consider the AGENTS.md guidance. The test section emphasizes: \"Test behavior that matters, not the shape of the implementation.\" and \"A test file that mirrors the source file function-for-function is usually testing that the code was typed.\" \n\nFor a diagnostic feature, a meaningful test would verify that the explain output correctly marks a Stream as fallback and a scalar as kernel. This is behavior that matters. Let me check how tests are organized.\n\nLet me look at the tests directory.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e206c210015KVyfAhawBvCo7","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","messageID":"msg_05e201f14001nYSEQaWRV2ZGuq"}}
