{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788285587327,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a05e20859e725286ae393f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/tests/reductions.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/tests/reductions.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! What the checker refuses about `sum` and `max` (kantord/toylang#140).\n2: \n3: /// `sum` is defined only for the two integer element types, so a scalar argument is refused\n4: /// with the restricted set named, the way `sort` names its own.\n5: #[test]\n6: fn sum_takes_a_vec() {\n7:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n8:         toylang::compile(r#\"sum(1)\"#)\n9:             .map(|_| ())\n10:             .unwrap_err()\n11:             .to_string()\n12:     );\n13: }\n14: \n15: /// Neither Str nor Char reduces (the ruling cut them on the same no-caller grounds it cut min\n16: /// and product), so a Vec of either is refused rather than silently ordered.\n17: #[test]\n18: fn sum_takes_an_int_element() {\n19:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n20:         toylang::compile(r#\"sum([\"a\"])\"#)\n21:             .map(|_| ())\n22:             .unwrap_err()\n23:             .to_string()\n24:     );\n25: }\n26: \n27: #[test]\n28: fn max_takes_a_vec() {\n29:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n30:         toylang::compile(r#\"max(1)\"#)\n31:             .map(|_| ())\n32:             .unwrap_err()\n33:             .to_string()\n34:     );\n35: }\n36: \n37: /// A record has no total order, so a Vec of records cannot be reduced to a maximum.\n38: #[test]\n39: fn max_takes_an_int_element() {\n40:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n41:         toylang::compile(r#\"max([{n: 1}])\"#)\n42:             .map(|_| ())\n43:             .unwrap_err()\n44:             .to_string()\n45:     );\n46: }\n47: \n48: /// A builtin is a reserved name: a program that defines `sum` and means something else by it\n49: /// is refused the same way `str` is, rather than silently shadowed.\n50: #[test]\n51: fn a_builtin_cannot_be_redefined() {\n52:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n53:         toylang::compile(\"fn sum(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = x[0]!\\n\\nsum([1])\")\n54:             .map(|_| ())\n55:             .unwrap_err()\n56:             .to_string()\n57:     );\n58: }\n\n(End of file - total 58 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! What the checker refuses about `sum` and `max` (kantord/toylang#140).\n\n/// `sum` is defined only for the two integer element types, so a scalar argument is refused\n/// with the restricted set named, the way `sort` names its own.\n#[test]\nfn sum_takes_a_vec() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(r#\"sum(1)\"#)\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// Neither Str nor Char reduces (the ruling cut them on the same no-caller grounds it cut min\n/// and product), so a Vec of either is refused rather than silently ordered.\n#[test]\nfn sum_takes_an_int_element() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(r#\"sum([\"a\"])\"#)","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/tests/reductions.rs","text":"//! What the checker refuses about `sum` and `max` (kantord/toylang#140).\n\n/// `sum` is defined only for the two integer element types, so a scalar argument is refused\n/// with the restricted set named, the way `sort` names its own.\n#[test]\nfn sum_takes_a_vec() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(r#\"sum(1)\"#)\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// Neither Str nor Char reduces (the ruling cut them on the same no-caller grounds it cut min\n/// and product), so a Vec of either is refused rather than silently ordered.\n#[test]\nfn sum_takes_an_int_element() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(r#\"sum([\"a\"])\"#)\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn max_takes_a_vec() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(r#\"max(1)\"#)\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// A record has no total order, so a Vec of records cannot be reduced to a maximum.\n#[test]\nfn max_takes_an_int_element() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(r#\"max([{n: 1}])\"#)\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// A builtin is a reserved name: a program that defines `sum` and means something else by it\n/// is refused the same way `str` is, rather than silently shadowed.\n#[test]\nfn a_builtin_cannot_be_redefined() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(\"fn sum(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = x[0]!\\n\\nsum([1])\")\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":58,"totalLines":58,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/reductions.rs","time":{"start":1788285587318,"end":1788285587325}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me look at how a test file is structured, e.g. tests/streams.rs or a simpler one, to understand the test conventions. Let me look at a small test file.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e2086140019wvlZsZuS8FbB3","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","messageID":"msg_05e206fa5001VNwcLslBCz5qFL"}}
