{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788285599059,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a05e20b7f67a5099b9fb0e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/tests/streams.rs","limit":60},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/tests/streams.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! Streaming input, in its most minimal cut: `lines`, `collect`, and the rules that keep a\n2: //! single-use stream from ending up somewhere it cannot be honoured.\n3: //!\n4: //! What every backend agrees on when it runs lives in the corpus, as `lines_cat.yaml` and its\n5: //! siblings. This file holds the claims a corpus case cannot express: that a program is\n6: //! refused, and why.\n7: \n8: #[track_caller]\n9: fn err(src: &str) -> String {\n10:     toylang::compile(src).map(|_| ()).unwrap_err().to_string()\n11: }\n12: \n13: /// The containment bans: a stream cannot end up stored inside a Vec, a record, an enum payload,\n14: /// or another stream, because nothing can get a single-use value back out of one once it is in.\n15: mod containment {\n16:     use super::err;\n17: \n18:     /// Nothing can get a `Lines` value back out of a Vec once it is in one, and there is only\n19:     /// ever one real stdin to hold in the first place.\n20:     #[test]\n21:     fn lines_cannot_enter_a_vec() {\n22:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"str(1) | [lines]\"));\n23:     }\n24: \n25:     /// Same reasoning as the Vec case, for a record field.\n26:     #[test]\n27:     fn lines_cannot_enter_a_record() {\n28:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"{a: lines}\"));\n29:     }\n30: \n31:     /// The containment bans hold in the type grammar itself, not just at value construction\n32:     /// sites: an annotation cannot describe a stream as stored in a Vec.\n33:     #[test]\n34:     fn a_signature_cannot_put_a_stream_in_a_vec() {\n35:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(v: Vec<Stream<Str>>) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n36:     }\n37: \n38:     /// Same ban for a record field, spelled in a signature.\n39:     #[test]\n40:     fn a_signature_cannot_put_a_stream_in_a_record() {\n41:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(r: {s: Stream<Str>}) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n42:     }\n43: \n44:     /// And for an enum variant's payload, the one other annotation a value constructor reads.\n45:     #[test]\n46:     fn an_enum_payload_cannot_hold_a_stream() {\n47:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { v{s: Stream<Str>} }\\n\\n1\"));\n48:     }\n49: \n50:     /// The parens spelling puts a type directly in payload position, so the ban has to hold\n51:     /// there too, not only inside a record.\n52:     #[test]\n53:     fn a_scalar_enum_payload_cannot_be_a_stream() {\n54:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { v(Stream<Str>) }\\n\\n1\"));\n55:     }\n56: \n57:     /// A stream of streams has nothing it could yield: its entries would not be values.\n58:     #[test]\n59:     fn a_stream_cannot_hold_another_stream() {\n60:         insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(s: Stream<Stream<Str>>) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n\n(Showing lines 1-60 of 296. Use offset=61 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! Streaming input, in its most minimal cut: `lines`, `collect`, and the rules that keep a\n//! single-use stream from ending up somewhere it cannot be honoured.\n//!\n//! What every backend agrees on when it runs lives in the corpus, as `lines_cat.yaml` and its\n//! siblings. This file holds the claims a corpus case cannot express: that a program is\n//! refused, and why.\n\n#[track_caller]\nfn err(src: &str) -> String {\n    toylang::compile(src).map(|_| ()).unwrap_err().to_string()\n}\n\n/// The containment bans: a stream cannot end up stored inside a Vec, a record, an enum payload,\n/// or another stream, because nothing can get a single-use value back out of one once it is in.\nmod containment {\n    use super::err;\n\n    /// Nothing can get a `Lines` value back out of a Vec once it is in one, and there is only\n    /// ever one real stdin to hold in the first place.\n    #[test]","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-168/tests/streams.rs","text":"//! Streaming input, in its most minimal cut: `lines`, `collect`, and the rules that keep a\n//! single-use stream from ending up somewhere it cannot be honoured.\n//!\n//! What every backend agrees on when it runs lives in the corpus, as `lines_cat.yaml` and its\n//! siblings. This file holds the claims a corpus case cannot express: that a program is\n//! refused, and why.\n\n#[track_caller]\nfn err(src: &str) -> String {\n    toylang::compile(src).map(|_| ()).unwrap_err().to_string()\n}\n\n/// The containment bans: a stream cannot end up stored inside a Vec, a record, an enum payload,\n/// or another stream, because nothing can get a single-use value back out of one once it is in.\nmod containment {\n    use super::err;\n\n    /// Nothing can get a `Lines` value back out of a Vec once it is in one, and there is only\n    /// ever one real stdin to hold in the first place.\n    #[test]\n    fn lines_cannot_enter_a_vec() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"str(1) | [lines]\"));\n    }\n\n    /// Same reasoning as the Vec case, for a record field.\n    #[test]\n    fn lines_cannot_enter_a_record() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"{a: lines}\"));\n    }\n\n    /// The containment bans hold in the type grammar itself, not just at value construction\n    /// sites: an annotation cannot describe a stream as stored in a Vec.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_signature_cannot_put_a_stream_in_a_vec() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(v: Vec<Stream<Str>>) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    /// Same ban for a record field, spelled in a signature.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_signature_cannot_put_a_stream_in_a_record() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(r: {s: Stream<Str>}) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    /// And for an enum variant's payload, the one other annotation a value constructor reads.\n    #[test]\n    fn an_enum_payload_cannot_hold_a_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { v{s: Stream<Str>} }\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    /// The parens spelling puts a type directly in payload position, so the ban has to hold\n    /// there too, not only inside a record.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_scalar_enum_payload_cannot_be_a_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { v(Stream<Str>) }\\n\\n1\"));\n    }\n\n    /// A stream of streams has nothing it could yield: its entries would not be values.\n    #[test]\n    fn a_stream_cannot_hold_another_stream() {\n        insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"fn f(s: Stream<Stream<Str>>) -> Int = 0\\n\\n1\"));","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":60,"totalLines":296,"truncated":true}},"title":"tests/streams.rs","time":{"start":1788285599055,"end":1788285599058}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Tests use `insta::assert_snapshot!`. Let me check how a test that exercises a library function (not just compile errors) is structured. Let me look at a test that runs a program and checks output, e.g. tests/step_1.rs or tests/streams.rs.\n\nActually, for the offload explain feature, a meaningful test would be: compile a program with a Stream and a scalar, call `toylang::offload::explain(&program)`, and snapshot the output. This tests the actual behavior of the diagnostic.\n\nLet me check how tests call library functions. Let me look at tests/streams.rs briefly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e20b86c001VicnwagRXmtUfa","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","messageID":"msg_05e2090af001XEQevg4lxlJJHj"}}
