{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788285638540,"sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a7051703db9a3c34","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cargo run --quiet -- --explain-offload emit scratch_off.toy lua 2>&1 | rg -v \"^warning|^ *-->|^ *\\||^ *\\^|^ *=\" | head -30"},"output":"169 | pub struct File {\n\n190 | pub struct Alias {\n\n87 | pub fn check(file: &File) -> Result<tir::Program, Error> {\n\n402 | fn check_program_body(ctx: &Ctx, body: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n\n242 | pub(super) fn prune_unreachable(funcs: Vec<tir::Func>, body: &Tir) -> Vec<tir::Func> {\n\n261 | fn calls_in(t: &Tir, out: &mut Vec<String>) {\n\n185 | pub(super) fn alias_map(aliases: &[Alias]) -> Result<Aliases<'_>, Error> {\n\n366 | pub fn parse(src: &str) -> Result<File, Error> {\n\n486 | impl<'i> Cursor<'i> {\n...\n496 |     fn peek2(&self) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n...\n824 |     fn alias(&mut self) -> Result<Alias, Error> {\n\n267 | pub struct Program {\n\n297 | pub fn vec_depth(ty: &Type) -> usize {\n\n309 | pub enum Stage<'a> {\n\n316 | pub enum Source {\n\n","metadata":{"output":"169 | pub struct File {\n\n190 | pub struct Alias {\n\n87 | pub fn check(file: &File) -> Result<tir::Program, Error> {\n\n402 | fn check_program_body(ctx: &Ctx, body: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n\n242 | pub(super) fn prune_unreachable(funcs: Vec<tir::Func>, body: &Tir) -> Vec<tir::Func> {\n\n261 | fn calls_in(t: &Tir, out: &mut Vec<String>) {\n\n185 | pub(super) fn alias_map(aliases: &[Alias]) -> Result<Aliases<'_>, Error> {\n\n366 | pub fn parse(src: &str) -> Result<File, Error> {\n\n486 | impl<'i> Cursor<'i> {\n...\n496 |     fn peek2(&self) -> Result<(Tok, Span), Error> {\n...\n824 |     fn alias(&mut self) -> Result<Alias, Error> {\n\n267 | pub struct Program {\n\n297 | pub fn vec_depth(ty: &Type) -> usize {\n\n309 | pub enum Stage<'a> {\n\n316 | pub enum Source {\n\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cargo run --quiet -- --explain-offload emit scratch_off.toy lua 2>&1 | rg -v \"^warning|^ *-->|^ *\\||^ *\\^|^ *=\" | head -30","time":{"start":1788285638536,"end":1788285638538}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I understand the test conventions. Tests use `toylang::compile(...)` and `insta::assert_snapshot!`.\n\nFor the offload explain feature, a meaningful test would be one that calls `toylang::offload::explain(&program)` and snapshots the output. Let me write a test file `tests/offload.rs` with a couple of cases:\n1. A program with a Stream (the scratch_off.toy example) - shows a stream falling back and a scalar kernel.\n2. Maybe a program with a Vec (Fold) and Opt.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to add tests. The AGENTS.md says \"Test behavior that matters\". The offload explain is a diagnostic whose behavior matters - whether it correctly identifies the fallback. A snapshot test over the explain output is exactly the right kind of test: it pins the diagnostic's behavior.\n\nLet me write the test. But first, let me think about whether `explain` is accessible from tests. It's `pub fn explain` in `src/offload.rs` which is `pub mod offload` in lib.rs. So `toylang::offload::explain(&program)` works.\n\nLet me write a test file. Let me use insta snapshots. Let me first check how insta snapshot tests are written - the snapshot gets stored in tests/snapshots/. Let me write the test file, then run it to generate .snap.new, then accept.\n\nActually wait - there's a \"KNOWN DENIAL: for snapshot updates run the plain test so insta writes .snap.new files, then cargo insta accept\". So I run the plain test, it writes .snap.new, then `cargo insta accept`. Good.\n\nLet me write the test file first.\n\nLet me design the test cases:\n\n1. A program with a stream that must fall back (scratch_off.toy):\n```toy\nfn double(xs: Stream<Int>) -> Stream<Int> =\n    xs | map(. * 2)\n\njsonlines(double(inputs))\n```\n\n2. A program with a Vec (Fold) reduction:\n```toy\nsum([1, 2, 3])\n```\nWait, sum returns Int, so the whole thing is One. Hmm. Let me think of a program where a Vec is involved as a fold.\n\nActually, let me think about a map over a Vec:\n```toy\njsonlines([1, 2, 3] | map(. * 2))\n```\nHmm, but jsonlines takes... let me check. jsonlines takes a Vec? Let me check what jsonlines expects.\n\nActually, let me not overcomplicate. Let me write a test that exercises:\n- a stream program (fallback)\n- a pure scalar program (all kernels)\n- maybe a vec fold\n\nLet me check the corpus for good examples. Actually, let me keep it simple. Let me write a test file with two or three cases that exercise the important behaviors.\n\nLet me first check what programs look like. Let me look at a corpus example with map over vec.\n\nActually, let me just write the tests and iterate. Let me write tests/offload.rs.\n\nLet me think about what programs compile and what they look like in explain output:\n\nCase 1: stream program (scratch_off.toy) - demonstrates fallback.\nCase 2: `sum([1, 2, 3])` - builtin call of a reduction. Let me predict the explain output... builtin : Int -- One -- offloadable kernel, with arg call : Vec<Int> -- Fold... hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe simpler to test a program like:\n```toy\n[1, 2, 3] | map(. + 1)\n```\nas the program body (result would be Vec<Int>). Actually let me check whether a bare Vec result is allowed... The program body result must be print-friendly. Let me just test and see.\n\nLet me write the test file with a couple cases and iterate on what compiles.\n\nLet me start with the stream case and a simple scalar program.\n\nActually, I want to be efficient. Let me write the test file, run it, see what fails, adjust.\n\nLet me write tests/offload.rs:","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e214a04001YTMnScXRuzHEgp","sessionID":"ses_fa1e30e50ffeU2O2jnEUGTDEg1","messageID":"msg_05e20be39001HHi5kYQ0TgYIPc"}}
